```c
#include "stm32f10x.h"

void delay_ms(uint32_t ms) {
    // Set prescaler to 8 (since 72MHz / 8 = 9MHz)
    RCC->APB1ENR |= RCC_APB1ENR_TIM4EN;
    TIM4->PSC = 71; // 72MHz / 71 = 9MHz

    // Set auto-reload value to 9999 (9999 * 9MHz = 8.999s)
    TIM4->ARR = 9999;

    // Enable update interrupt
    TIM4->DIER |= TIM_DIER_UIE;

    // Start counter
    TIM4->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;

    // Wait until update interrupt flag is set
    while (!(TIM4->SR & TIM_SR_UIF));

    // Clear update interrupt flag
    TIM4->SR &= ~TIM_SR_UIF;

    // Stop counter
    TIM4->CR1 &= ~TIM_CR1_CEN;
}

int main(void) {
    // Initialize GPIOA pin 5 as output
    RCC->AHB2ENR |= RCC_AHB2ENR_GPIOAEN;
    GPIOA->MODER |= GPIO_MODER_MODE5_0;

    // Call delay function
    delay_ms(2000);

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, we first enable the clock for the TIM4 peripheral by setting the `RCC_APB1ENR` register. We then set the prescaler to 8, which means that the timer will count at 9MHz. We set the auto-reload value to 9999, which means that the timer will count from 0 to 9999 before resetting. We enable the update interrupt by setting the `TIM_DIER` register. We start the counter by setting the `TIM_CR1` register. We wait until the update interrupt flag is set, which indicates that the timer has counted to 9999. We clear the update interrupt flag by clearing the `TIM_SR` register. Finally, we stop the counter by clearing the `TIM_CR1` register.

